#!/usr/bin/env bash
# smoke-leak-check — cycle the editor under `leaks --atExit` and assert nothing this project owns survives
# Author: Christopher Boone
# Date: 2026-07-30
#
# The GUI smoke harness proves the editor opens. This proves that opening and
# closing it several hundred times leaves nothing behind, which the harness
# cannot prove about itself: a leak it could see is one it already owns, so the
# measurement has to come from outside the process. See ADR 0013.
#
# `leaks` exits non-zero whenever it finds anything at all, and it always finds
# something: AppKit's LaunchServices chatter leaks a few hundred NSXPCConnection
# allocations per run, and none of them are this project's. So the exit code is
# not the signal, and neither is a clean grep on its own. The order of the
# assertions below is the point:
#
#   1. The harness itself passed. A leak report over a failed run means nothing.
#   2. `leaks` produced a report and it parses. A grep that finds nothing in
#      output that was never generated reads exactly like a pass.
#   3. No leaked object belongs to this project.
#   4. The total leaked bytes are inside MAXIMUM_LEAKED_BYTES.
#
# The patterns in PLUGIN_OWNED are prefixes, chosen from evidence rather than
# from the framework headers. Dropping the release of the command queue in
# `Renderer.deinit` leaks an object that `leaks` names `AGXG17XFamilyCommandQueue`,
# not `MTLCommandQueue`: the concrete Metal classes are driver-private and their
# names carry the GPU family. A list of the public class names would have
# reported that planted leak as clean.
#
# **Assertion 4 is there because assertion 3 is an allowlist of Objective-C class
# names, and this project's own allocations do not have one.** The plugin
# allocates through `std.heap.c_allocator`, so its blocks are on the malloc heap
# and `leaks` does find them, but a block carrying no class is not written as
# `<ClassName 0xADDRESS>` and the extracted token is not something PLUGIN_OWNED
# can match. Measured rather than reasoned about: dropping `self.history.deinit`
# from `plugin.destroy` and running 40 cycles leaks **roughly 42.6 million bytes
# against a clean 18,816**, and assertion 3 called it clean both times it was
# planted. That is two thousand times the baseline reported as nothing at all.
#
# **The count is not the discriminator and must not be used as one.** Across two
# runs of that same planted leak the report gave *232* leaks and then *328*,
# against a clean count of 288 that does not move at all — so the count wanders
# in both directions while forty megabytes go missing, and one of those two runs
# would have read as an improvement. The byte total moved to 42.6 million both
# times. Bytes are what moved; the count is noise with a leak underneath it.
#
# Usage:
#   smoke-leak-check [--cycles N] <path/to/fosforo-smoke>
#
#   --cycles N   Open and close the editor N times. Defaults to 400.
#
# Exit codes:
#   0  - The harness passed and leaked nothing this project owns
#  64  - Invalid arguments
#  65  - Something leaked
#  66  - The harness binary does not exist or is not executable
#  69  - The harness failed or the report did not parse, so nothing was measured
#
# The last two are separate for a reason that arrived with CI. This runs under
# `continue-on-error`, where nobody opens the log of a step that cannot block a
# merge, so the exit code is the whole message. "This project leaked something"
# and "the instrument did not run" are the two conclusions ADR 0013's assertion
# order exists to keep apart, and returning one code for both put them back
# together at exactly the moment it started to matter.

set -euo pipefail

if [[ -n "${TRACE:-}" ]]; then
  set -x
fi

readonly E_USAGE=64
readonly E_DATAERR=65
readonly E_NOINPUT=66
readonly E_UNAVAILABLE=69

readonly PROGRAM_NAME="${0##*/}"

# How deep a by-hand run goes. **Not what CI runs**, which passes 40 through
# `zig build smoke-leaks -Dleak-cycles=40`, because the harness waits on vsync
# about thirteen times per cycle and a hosted runner refreshes at half this
# machine's rate: 400 cycles costs 113 s here and around two minutes there.
#
# This constant used to be justified by an argument about totals — "at 400 a
# per-cycle leak more than doubles the total; at 10 it would sit inside the
# run-to-run variation" — and that argument is wrong twice. Neither assertion
# here judges the count, and the baseline barely varies with the count at all:
# across five runs at 40 and at 400 cycles the figure was 288 leaks for 18,816
# bytes four times and 285 for 18,464 once, so a tenfold change in depth moves it
# by under 2% and only run to run. AppKit's chatter is a fixed startup cost rather
# than a per-cycle one. What depth actually buys is repetition of the rare paths
# inside `oneCycle`: the every-fourth-cycle teardown through `plugin.destroy`,
# and the four resizes including the wrapped-negative height REAPER sends. 40 reaches
# those 10 and 40 times, which is why it is enough for CI, and 400 is kept here
# because a by-hand run is not paying for a runner.
readonly DEFAULT_CYCLES=400

# Where the report starts. Also the positive control: no such line means `leaks`
# produced nothing, and every check after it would pass vacuously.
readonly REPORT_MARKER='leaks Report Version'

# The ceiling on total leaked bytes, whatever the classes say.
#
# One mebibyte, against a baseline that sits between 18,464 and 18,816 bytes
# across six runs at 40 and at 400 cycles on this machine.
# **It is deliberately loose, and that looseness is the property that makes it
# work on a machine nobody has a shell on.** It sits 56 times above the observed
# baseline, so a hosted runner's own AppKit chatter has two orders of magnitude
# to differ by before it matters, and 40 times below the smallest leak it is for:
# the history ring is 1 MiB and one is dropped per cycle, so even a single cycle
# would clear this. A threshold with that much room on both sides does not need
# calibrating against the machine it runs on, which is exactly what a peak-RSS
# slope would have needed and could not have (#63).
#
# **The runner is noisier than this machine, and the looseness is what absorbs
# it.** Nine CI runs reported 18,656, 18,816, 14,080, 18,816, 14,080, 9,728,
# 18,624, 18,624 and 18,624 bytes: a low of 9,728 against a high of 18,816.
# **Quote that as a ratio, not a percentage.** This comment used to say "a 25%
# spread" over the first three runs, which is (max-min)/max; the naive
# (max-min)/min gives 93% for the same nine runs and is not the same measure.
# As a ratio the first three were 1.34x and nine are 1.93x, against the 2% seen
# across six runs here. The figure widened as the sample grew, which is the
# argument for the looseness rather than against it, and it is why `smoke-leaks`
# keeps `continue-on-error` where `smoke-appkit` no longer does (#72). Anything
# tight enough to be called calibrated would have to be re-measured against
# that, and again whenever the image refreshed; this has 107x of room at the low
# end and 56x at the high end, so it does not.
readonly MAXIMUM_LEAKED_BYTES=1048576

# Class-name prefixes this project is responsible for, as `leaks` spells them.
readonly PLUGIN_OWNED='^(NSView|CALayer|CAMetalLayer|IOSurface|IOGPU|_?MTL|AGX)'

# Write a message to stderr, prefixed with the program name.
# Arguments:
#   $@ - message parts
function warn() {
  echo "${PROGRAM_NAME}: ${*}" >&2
}

function usage() {
  echo "Usage: ${PROGRAM_NAME} [--cycles N] <path/to/fosforo-smoke>" >&2
}

# Everything from the report marker to the end of the capture.
# Arguments:
#   $1 - path to the captured output
# Outputs:
#   The report on stdout, empty when `leaks` produced none
function report_body() {
  local capture="${1}"

  sed -n "/${REPORT_MARKER}/,\$p" "${capture}"
}

# The total leaked bytes the summary line reports.
#
# `sed` rather than `grep`, deliberately. Under `errexit` a `grep` that matches
# nothing takes the script down where the number is read rather than where it is
# judged, which reads as a crash instead of a diagnosis, and `grep ... || true`
# would hand back the empty string — which bash reads as zero in an arithmetic
# context, and zero passes. `sed -n ... p` exits zero either way and prints one
# line per match, so the caller can reject two matches as easily as none.
# Arguments:
#   $1 - path to the captured output
# Outputs:
#   One line per matching line, which must be exactly one
function leaked_bytes() {
  local capture="${1}"

  sed -n 's/.*for \([0-9]\{1,\}\) total leaked bytes.*/\1/p' "${capture}"
}

# The class of every leaked object, one per line, deduplicated.
#
# `leaks` writes each entry as `<ClassName 0xADDRESS>`, so anchoring on the angle
# bracket is what separates a leaked object's class from the same name appearing
# in a stack frame. Without that anchor the binary image names in the backtraces
# match too, and the report always carries several.
# Arguments:
#   $1 - path to the captured output
# Outputs:
#   One class name per line
function leaked_classes() {
  local capture="${1}"

  report_body "${capture}" |
    grep -oE '<[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*' |
    tr -d '<' |
    sort -u
}

function main() {
  local binary=""
  local cycles="${DEFAULT_CYCLES}"
  local capture
  local summary
  local bytes
  local classes
  local offender
  local offenders
  local -i status

  while [[ ${#} -gt 0 ]]; do
    case "${1}" in
      --cycles)
        if [[ ${#} -lt 2 ]]; then
          warn "--cycles needs a count"
          usage
          return "${E_USAGE}"
        fi
        cycles="${2}"
        shift
        ;;
      -*)
        warn "unknown option '${1}'"
        usage
        return "${E_USAGE}"
        ;;
      *)
        if [[ -n "${binary}" ]]; then
          warn "expected exactly one harness path"
          usage
          return "${E_USAGE}"
        fi
        binary="${1}"
        ;;
    esac
    shift
  done

  if [[ -z "${binary}" ]]; then
    usage
    return "${E_USAGE}"
  fi

  if [[ ! -x "${binary}" ]]; then
    warn "cannot execute '${binary}'"
    return "${E_NOINPUT}"
  fi

  if ! [[ "${cycles}" =~ ^[1-9][0-9]*$ ]]; then
    warn "'${cycles}' is not a positive cycle count"
    return "${E_USAGE}"
  fi

  capture="$(mktemp -t smoke-leak-check)"
  # shellcheck disable=SC2064 # Expand now: the path must survive the function scope.
  trap "rm -f '${capture}'" EXIT

  warn "running ${cycles} cycles under leaks --atExit, please wait"

  # MallocStackLogging is what makes the report name the allocation site rather
  # than an address. `leaks` exits non-zero on any leak at all, including the
  # ones AppKit produces on its own, so its status is recorded and not obeyed.
  status=0
  MallocStackLogging=1 leaks --atExit -- "${binary}" appkit "${cycles}" > "${capture}" 2>&1 || status=${?}

  # First, because a leak report over a run that fell over partway through
  # describes a process that never reached the teardown being measured.
  if ! grep -q '^smoke: appkit ok$' "${capture}"; then
    warn "the harness did not pass, so there is nothing to measure"
    tail -n 20 "${capture}" >&2
    return "${E_UNAVAILABLE}"
  fi

  # Second. Everything below is a search for absence, and a search for absence
  # over output that was never produced succeeds for the wrong reason.
  summary="$(grep -E '[0-9]+ leaks for [0-9]+ total leaked bytes' "${capture}" || true)"
  if [[ -z "$(report_body "${capture}")" || -z "${summary}" ]]; then
    warn "leaks produced no parseable report (exit ${status}); the checks below would be vacuous"
    tail -n 20 "${capture}" >&2
    return "${E_UNAVAILABLE}"
  fi

  classes="$(leaked_classes "${capture}")"
  if [[ -z "${classes}" ]]; then
    warn "the report names no leaked classes at all, which the summary contradicts"
    return "${E_UNAVAILABLE}"
  fi

  # The anchored pattern rejects two matches as well as none: `sed` prints one
  # line per match, `$(...)` strips only the trailing newline, and this test has
  # no multiline flag, so a two-line result fails it the same way an empty one
  # does. Three failure modes, one assertion, and no way to satisfy it vacuously.
  bytes="$(leaked_bytes "${capture}")"
  if ! [[ "${bytes}" =~ ^[0-9]+$ ]]; then
    warn "no single 'total leaked bytes' figure in the report; the check below would be vacuous"
    tail -n 20 "${capture}" >&2
    return "${E_UNAVAILABLE}"
  fi

  warn "leaks: ${summary#*: }"
  warn "report parsed, $(echo "${classes}" | wc -l | tr -d ' ') distinct leaked classes"

  # Third, and before the byte bound rather than after it, because this one can
  # say *what* leaked. A dropped pipeline-state release takes the total to
  # 8,555,776 bytes, which is over the bound as well, and "these objects belong
  # to this project, here they are" is a strictly better first thing to read than
  # "some number of bytes went missing". The bound is the catch-all for what this
  # cannot name, so it goes second.
  offenders="$(echo "${classes}" | grep -E "${PLUGIN_OWNED}" || true)"
  if [[ -n "${offenders}" ]]; then
    warn "these leaked objects belong to this project:"
    while IFS= read -r offender; do
      echo "  ${offender}" >&2
    done <<< "${offenders}"
    return "${E_DATAERR}"
  fi

  # Fourth, and the one that reaches allocations with no Objective-C class. See
  # the header: a dropped `history.deinit` leaks tens of megabytes and the check
  # above calls it clean, because a malloc block is not written as `<ClassName>`.
  #
  # **The message says what this knows rather than what it suspects.** Reaching
  # here means no class matched `PLUGIN_OWNED`, and a classless block is only the
  # likelier of two readings: the other is a leaked class the allowlist does not
  # name, which is the exact mistake the `AGXG17XFamilyCommandQueue` note in the
  # header records somebody already making once. Both are named.
  if [[ "${bytes}" -gt "${MAXIMUM_LEAKED_BYTES}" ]]; then
    warn "${bytes} bytes leaked, above the ${MAXIMUM_LEAKED_BYTES}-byte bound"
    warn "no leaked class matched PLUGIN_OWNED, which is consistent with two different things"
    warn "  an allocation carrying no class at all, which is what a planted history-ring leak looks like"
    warn "  a leaked class this allowlist does not name, the way MTLCommandQueue did not name AGXG17XFamilyCommandQueue"
    warn "check the report's largest blocks before assuming the first; the classes above are what it saw"
    return "${E_DATAERR}"
  fi

  warn "nothing this project owns was leaked, and ${bytes} bytes is inside the ${MAXIMUM_LEAKED_BYTES}-byte bound"
}

main "${@}"
